<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page4-zero-sum" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Maximins and Two-Player Zero-Sum Games</title>

  <p>
    Later on this page, we'll introduce <em>zero-sum games</em>. But first,
    we'll work out what a <q>maximin</q> is. It turns out to have an
    important connection to zero-sum games and their Nash equilibria.
  </p>

  <p>
    We'll go through maximins here in the notes first. The same material is
    also in the book, and you'll skim through it in the first reading block later on
    this page.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 1: maximin notation                                     -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Suppose Player 1 commits to a strategy <m>\sigma_1</m>. What's the worst
    that could happen to them? Player 2 could play anything, so we look at
    the smallest payoff Player 1 could get as we range over all of Player 2's
    possible strategies <m>\sigma_2</m>:
    <me>
      m_1(\sigma_1) = \min_{\sigma_2} \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)
    </me>
    So if Player 1 plays <m>\sigma_1</m>, then <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m> is the
    smallest possible payoff they can get.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's try it on a small example. Here are Player 1's payoffs for two of
    their strategies, Top and Bottom, against each of Player 2's three pure
    strategies. (Only Player 1's payoffs are shown.)
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-maximin-example">
    <title>Player 1's payoffs for Top and Bottom</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell>Player 2 plays L</cell>
        <cell>Player 2 plays M</cell>
        <cell>Player 2 plays R</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1 plays Top</cell>
        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1 plays Bottom</cell>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <exercise xml:id="check-maximin-worst-cases">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find Player 1's worst case for each of their two strategies.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>m_1(\text{Top}) =</m>
        <fillin name="mtop" answer="-1" width="3"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>m_1(\text{Bottom}) =</m>
        <fillin name="mbot" answer="0" width="3"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate name="mtop">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The worst thing Player 2 can do to them is play M.
              What about mixed strategies for Player 2, since <m>\sigma_2</m>
              ranges over all of them? A mixed strategy gives Player 1 a
              weighted average of <m>3</m>, <m>-1</m>, and <m>2</m>, and an
              average can't be lower than the smallest number being averaged.
              So the worst case is always at one of Player 2's pure
              strategies. Keep this in mind, because it comes up again soon.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="3"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the <em>best</em> case for Top. We want the worst case,
              the smallest payoff Player 2 could leave them with.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="1.3333" tolerance="0.01"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the average of Top's three payoffs, which is what Player
              1 would get if Player 2 picked each column equally often. But
              <m>m_1</m> asks about the worst Player 2 could do to them, not a
              typical outcome.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000" max="-1.001"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Can Player 2 push them below <m>-1</m>? Even with a mixed
              strategy, Player 1 gets a weighted average of <m>3</m>,
              <m>-1</m>, and <m>2</m>, and an average can't be lower than the
              smallest number being averaged.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Look for the smallest payoff in the Top row. That's the worst
            thing Player 2 can do to them.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="mbot">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-1"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>-1</m> is the smallest number in the whole table, but it's
              in the Top row. <m>m_1(\text{Bottom})</m> only looks at what
              happens when Player 1 plays Bottom.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="2"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the <em>best</em> case for Bottom. We want the worst
              case.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="1"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the average of Bottom's three payoffs. But <m>m_1</m>
              asks about the worst Player 2 could do to them, not a typical
              outcome.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Look for the smallest payoff in the Bottom row.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now suppose Player 1 is risk-averse, so they want to choose a strategy
    <m>\sigma_1</m> that makes the worst-case scenario as good as possible.
    More precisely, they want the <m>\sigma_1</m> with the largest
    <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m>. That's called a <term>maximin strategy</term> for
    Player 1, which we denote <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m>. The corresponding
    <term>maximin payoff</term> is written <m>m_1^\dagger</m>:
    <me>
      m_1^\dagger = m_1(\sigma_1^\dagger) = \max_{\sigma_1} m_1(\sigma_1)
      = \max_{\sigma_1} \min_{\sigma_2} \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)
    </me>
    By playing <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m>, Player 1 guarantees themselves at
    least <m>m_1^\dagger</m> no matter what Player 2 does, and no other
    strategy guarantees them more.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-maximin-example-strategy">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Go back to the payoffs in <xref ref="table-maximin-example"/>. You
        found <m>m_1(\text{Top}) = -1</m> and <m>m_1(\text{Bottom}) = 0</m>.
        If Player 1 is choosing between Top and Bottom, what is their maximin
        strategy, and what is their maximin payoff?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Top, with maximin payoff <m>3</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>3</m> is Top's <em>best</em> case. A maximin strategy compares
            worst cases: <m>m_1(\text{Top}) = -1</m> and
            <m>m_1(\text{Bottom}) = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Top, with maximin payoff <m>-1</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Top's worst case is <m>-1</m>, but Bottom's worst case is
            better. The maximin strategy is the one with the
            <em>largest</em> worst case.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Bottom, with maximin payoff <m>0</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Bottom's worst case (<m>0</m>) beats Top's
            (<m>-1</m>), so by playing Bottom, Player 1 guarantees at least
            <m>0</m>. Could a mix of Top and Bottom do even better? Not here:
            against M, any mix earns at most <m>0</m>, since Top gets
            <m>-1</m> and Bottom gets <m>0</m> there.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Bottom, with maximin payoff <m>2</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Bottom is the right strategy, but <m>2</m> is its best case. The
            maximin payoff is the worst case of the maximin strategy.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    We can define the same things for Player 2: her worst case
    <m>m_2(\sigma_2)</m> for each strategy <m>\sigma_2</m>, her maximin
    strategy <m>\sigma_2^\dagger</m>, and her maximin payoff
    <m>m_2^\dagger</m>. Before you look at the book's version, see if you can
    figure out what the formula should be.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-maximin-player2-formula">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Build the formula for Player 2's maximin payoff
        <m>m_2^\dagger</m> by putting the blocks in order. Not every block
        belongs! (The blocks can't show typeset math, so
        <c>max_σ₂</c> stands for <m>\displaystyle\max_{\sigma_2}</m>, and
        so on.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks layout="horizontal">
      <block>m₂† =</block>
      <block>max_σ₂</block>
      <block>min_σ₁</block>
      <block>π₂(σ₁, σ₂)</block>
      <block correct="no">max_σ₁</block>
      <block correct="no">min_σ₂</block>
      <block correct="no">π₁(σ₁, σ₂)</block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Three questions: Whose payoff does Player 2 care about? Which
        strategy does Player 2 get to choose (that's the max)? And whose
        choices make up her worst case (that's the min)?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle m_2^\dagger = \max_{\sigma_2} \min_{\sigma_1}
        \pi_2(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>. For each of her strategies
        <m>\sigma_2</m>, Player 2 finds her worst case over everything
        Player 1 could do. Then she picks the <m>\sigma_2</m> with the best
        worst case. The common mistakes are using <m>\pi_1</m> (Player 2
        cares about <em>her</em> payoff) and swapping which player the max
        and min range over.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Finally, a strategy profile <m>(\sigma_1^\dagger, \sigma_2^\dagger)</m>
    in which <em>both</em> players use maximin strategies is called a
    <term>maximin equilibrium</term>. In a maximin equilibrium, each player
    gets at least their maximin payoff <m>m_i^\dagger</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 2: Battle of the Sexes walkthrough                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Let's find the maximin equilibrium for a game we already know, the
    Battle of the Sexes from Section 3.2.4. Alice and Bob each choose
    between a concert and a wrestling match. Here's the payoff matrix with
    their probabilities in the margins.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bos-pq">
    <title>Battle of the Sexes, with mixed strategies</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Bob</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-q}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell>concert</cell>
        <cell>wrestling</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Alice</cell>
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell>concert</cell>
        <cell>(2, 1)</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-p}</m></cell>
        <cell>wrestling</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(1, 2)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    So Alice's mixed strategy is
    <m>\sigma_1 = p \cdot \text{concert} + (1-p) \cdot \text{wrestling}</m>,
    and Bob's is
    <m>\sigma_2 = q \cdot \text{concert} + (1-q) \cdot \text{wrestling}</m>.
    Like the book, we'll write a strategy profile as <m>(p, q)</m> instead of
    <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>. Alice's expected payoff is
    <me>
      \pi_1(p, q) = 2 \cdot pq + 0 \cdot p(1-q) + 0 \cdot (1-p)q
      + 1 \cdot (1-p)(1-q),
    </me>
    which simplifies to
    <me>
      \pi_1(p, q) = (1 - p) + (3p - 1)q.
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Each fixed value of <m>p</m> corresponds to one of Alice's strategies,
    <m>\sigma_1 = p \cdot \text{concert} + (1-p) \cdot \text{wrestling}</m>.
    So for each <m>p</m>, we can find Alice's worst case:
    <me>
      m_1(p) = \min_{0 \leq q \leq 1} \pi_1(p, q).
    </me>
    To find it, we hold <m>p</m> fixed and look for the <m>q</m> that
    minimizes <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m>. Differentiating with respect to <m>q</m>:
    <me>
      \frac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q} = 3p - 1, \quad\text{which is }
      \begin{cases}
        \text{negative} \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}, \\
        0 \amp \text{if } p = \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \text{positive} \amp \text{if } \frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>
    For a fixed <m>p</m>, the graph of <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> is a line in
    <m>q</m> with slope <m>3p - 1</m>, and the sign of that slope tells us
    where the line is lowest as <m>q</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>.
    Here are the graphs for one sample value of <m>p</m> from each of the
    three cases.
  </p>

  <!-- The three graphs are static files: assets/ch5-graph/bos-pi1-family-graphs.svg
       (web) and .pdf (print). They were made from the TikZ code below. To change
       the graphs, edit this code, compile it, and replace both files.

        \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\footnotesize]
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelA,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$}, ylabel={$\pi_1(p,q)$},
          title style={align=center},
          title={$p = 0.2$\\{\scriptsize like every $p$ with $0 \le p &lt; \frac{1}{3}$}},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, very thick] {0.8 - 0.4*x};
        \end{axis}
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelB,
          at={(panelA.south east)}, anchor=south west, xshift=1.1cm,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$},
          title style={align=center},
          title={$p = \frac{1}{3}$\\{\scriptsize the only $p$ in its case}},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, very thick] {2/3};
          \node[anchor=north, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
            at (axis cs:0.5,0.55) {every $q$ gives $\frac{2}{3}$};
        \end{axis}
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelC,
          at={(panelB.south east)}, anchor=south west, xshift=1.1cm,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$},
          title style={align=center},
          title={$p = 0.75$\\{\scriptsize like every $p$ with $\frac{1}{3} &lt; p \le 1$}},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, very thick] {0.25 + 1.25*x};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
        -->
    <figure xml:id="fig-bos-pi1-three-p">
    <caption>
      Alice's payoff <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> as a function of Bob's <m>q</m>, for
      one sample value of <m>p</m> from each case: <m>p = 0.2</m>,
      <m>p = \frac{1}{3}</m>, and <m>p = 0.75</m>. Each graph looks like
      every other graph in its case. For example, every <m>p</m> with
      <m>0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}</m> gives a line shaped like the one for
      <m>p = 0.2</m>.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="img-bos-pi1-family-graphs" width="100%">
      <shortdescription>
        Three graphs of Alice's payoff against Bob's probability q: decreasing
        for p equals 0.2, flat for p equals one third, and increasing for p
        equals 0.75.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          Three side-by-side graphs. Each has Bob's probability q of going to
          the concert on the horizontal axis, from 0 to 1, and Alice's
          expected payoff on the vertical axis, from 0 to 1.6.
        </p>
        <p>
          Left graph, p equals 0.2, labeled as looking like every p that is
          at least 0 and less than one third: a straight line decreasing from
          0.8 at q equals 0 down to 0.4 at q equals 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          Middle graph, p equals one third, labeled as the only p in its
          case: a horizontal line at height two thirds. Every value of q
          gives the same payoff, two thirds.
        </p>
        <p>
          Right graph, p equals 0.75, labeled as looking like every p that is
          greater than one third and at most 1: a straight line increasing
          from 0.25 at q equals 0 up to 1.5 at q equals 1.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\footnotesize]
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelA,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$}, ylabel={$\pi_1(p,q)$},
          title style={align=center},
          title={$p = 0.2$\\{\scriptsize like every $p$ with $0 \le p &lt; \frac{1}{3}$}},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, samples=2, very thick] {0.8 - 0.4*x};
        \end{axis}
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelB,
          at={(panelA.south east)}, anchor=south west, xshift=1.1cm,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$},
          title style={align=center},
          title={$p = \frac{1}{3}$\\{\scriptsize the only $p$ in its case}},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, samples=2, very thick] {2/3};
          \node[anchor=north, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
            at (axis cs:0.5,0.55) {every $q$ gives $\frac{2}{3}$};
        \end{axis}
        \begin{axis}[
          name=panelC,
          at={(panelB.south east)}, anchor=south west, xshift=1.1cm,
          width=5.4cm, height=4.6cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.05, ymin=0, ymax=1.65,
          xtick={0,0.5,1}, ytick={0,0.5,1,1.5},
          xlabel={$q$},
          title style={align=center},
          title={$p = 0.75$\\{\scriptsize like every $p$ with $\frac{1}{3} &lt; p \le 1$}},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0:1, samples=2, very thick] {0.25 + 1.25*x};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-bos-worst-case-fillin">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the graphs to find the value of <m>q</m> that gives Alice her
        lowest payoff (her worst case) for each sample value of <m>p</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.2</m>: <m>q =</m>
        <fillin name="q02" answer="1" width="3"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = \frac{1}{3}</m>: the line is flat, so every <m>q</m> gives
        Alice the same payoff, <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. Any <m>q</m> is a worst
        case, and <m>m_1(\frac{1}{3}) = \frac{2}{3}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.75</m>: <m>q =</m>
        <fillin name="q075" answer="0" width="3"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate name="q02">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="0"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              At <m>q = 0</m> the line is at its <em>highest</em>, Alice's
              best case. The line is decreasing, so where is it lowest?
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The line for <m>p = 0.2</m> is decreasing, so it's lowest at one
            of the ends.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="q075">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="1"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              At <m>q = 1</m> the line is at its <em>highest</em>. This time
              the line is increasing, so where is it lowest?
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The line for <m>p = 0.75</m> is increasing, so it's lowest at one
            of the ends.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.2</m>, the line is decreasing, so it's lowest at
        <m>q = 1</m>. Alice's worst case is Bob always going to the concert:
        she's usually at the wrestling match, so the worst thing for her is
        Bob being somewhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>p = 0.75</m>, the line is increasing, so it's lowest at
        <m>q = 0</m>. Now she's usually at the concert, so her worst case is
        Bob always going to the wrestling match.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The same reasoning works for <em>every</em> <m>p</m>, not just these
    three. If <m>0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}</m>, the slope <m>3p - 1</m> is
    negative, so the line is lowest at <m>q = 1</m>. If
    <m>\frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1</m>, the slope is positive, so the line is
    lowest at <m>q = 0</m>. Putting the three cases together:
    <me>
      m_1(p) = \min_{0 \leq q \leq 1} \pi_1(p, q) =
      \begin{cases}
        \pi_1(p, 1) \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \text{any } \pi_1(p, q) \amp \text{if } p = \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \pi_1(p, 0) \amp \text{if } \frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>
    Now plug in. Using <m>\pi_1(p, q) = (1 - p) + (3p - 1)q</m>, we get
    <m>\pi_1(p, 1) = 2p</m> and <m>\pi_1(p, 0) = 1 - p</m>, and every
    <m>\pi_1(\frac{1}{3}, q)</m> equals <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. So
    <me>
      m_1(p) =
      \begin{cases}
        2p \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{1}{3}, \\
        \frac{2}{3} \amp \text{if } p = \frac{1}{3}, \\
        1 - p \amp \text{if } \frac{1}{3} \lt p \leq 1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Here's the graph of the piecewise function <m>m_1(p)</m>:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bos-m1-graph">
    <caption>
      Alice's worst case <m>m_1(p)</m> (solid), the lower of the lines
      <m>2p</m> and <m>1 - p</m>. The dashed segments show where each line
      continues above the other.
    </caption>
    <image width="70%">
      <shortdescription>
        Graph of Alice's worst case m1 of p: rising along 2p up to a peak of
        two thirds at p equals one third, then falling along 1 minus p.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A graph with Alice's probability p of going to the concert on the
          horizontal axis, from 0 to 1, and her worst-case payoff on the
          vertical axis, from 0 to 1.2. The horizontal axis is marked at 0,
          one third, and 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          The solid graph of m1 of p is shaped like an upside-down V. From p
          equals 0 to p equals one third, it follows the line 2p, rising from
          0 to two thirds. From p equals one third to p equals 1, it follows
          the line 1 minus p, falling from two thirds to 0. The peak, at the
          point one third, two thirds, is marked with a dot.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dashed segments show the line 2p continuing upward past the peak
          and the line 1 minus p continuing upward to the left of the peak.
          In both cases the dashed part is above the solid graph, showing
          that m1 of p is always the lower of the two lines.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small]
        \begin{axis}[
          width=10cm, height=7cm,
          axis lines=left,
          xmin=0, xmax=1.08, ymin=0, ymax=1.25,
          xtick={0,0.3333,1}, xticklabels={$0$,$\frac{1}{3}$,$1$},
          ytick={0,0.6667,1}, yticklabels={$0$,$\frac{2}{3}$,$1$},
          xlabel={$p$}, ylabel={$m_1(p)$},
          clip=false
        ]
          \addplot[domain=0.3333:0.58, dashed, thick, gray] {2*x};
          \addplot[domain=0:0.3333, dashed, thick, gray] {1 - x};
          \addplot[domain=0:0.3333, very thick] {2*x};
          \addplot[domain=0.3333:1, very thick] {1 - x};
          \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.6pt] coordinates {(0.3333,0.6667)};
          \node[anchor=west, font=\footnotesize] (peaklabel) at (axis cs:0.62,0.86)
            {peak $\left(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3}\right)$};
          \draw[-&gt;, thin] (peaklabel.west) -- (axis cs:0.355,0.685);
          \node[anchor=north west, font=\footnotesize] at (axis cs:0.13,0.25)
            {$m_1(p) = 2p$};
          \node[anchor=south west, font=\footnotesize] at (axis cs:0.68,0.33)
            {$m_1(p) = 1 - p$};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Remember, a maximin strategy is the one whose worst case is as good as
    possible. In other words, Alice's maximin strategy <m>p^\dagger</m> is
    the value of <m>p</m> that makes <m>m_1(p)</m> as large as possible.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-bos-maximin-p">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the graph, what is Alice's maximin strategy <m>p^\dagger</m>,
        and what is her maximin payoff <m>m_1^\dagger</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = 1</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = 2</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The concert is Alice's favorite, and <m>2</m> is her best
            possible payoff. But if she always goes to the concert, Bob could
            go to the wrestling match, and she'd get <m>0</m>. In fact
            <m>m_1(1) = 0</m>, the lowest point on the graph!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = \frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The highest point of <m>m_1(p)</m> is the peak at
            <m>(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3})</m>. By going to the concert with
            probability <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>, Alice guarantees herself an
            expected payoff of at least <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, whatever Bob does.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = \frac{1}{3}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Read the peak off the graph again: the first coordinate is
            <m>p^\dagger</m> and the second is <m>m_1^\dagger</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p^\dagger = 0</m> and <m>m_1^\dagger = 1</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            If Alice always goes to the wrestling match, Bob could go to the
            concert, and she'd get <m>0</m>. So <m>m_1(0) = 0</m>, not
            <m>1</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    A similar calculation for Bob shows that his maximin strategy is
    <m>q^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m>, and his maximin payoff is
    <m>m_2^\dagger = \frac{2}{3}</m>. So
    <m>(p^\dagger, q^\dagger) = (\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3})</m> is a maximin
    equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now, this maximin equilibrium <m>(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{2}{3})</m> is
    <em>not</em> a Nash equilibrium. (If you want, try to figure out why!)
    But there is a kind of game where maximin equilibria and Nash equilibria
    line up exactly, and that's where we're headed next.
  </p>

  <p>
    A two-player game is <term>zero-sum</term> if the two players' payoffs
    add up to <m>0</m> in every cell of the payoff matrix: whatever one
    player wins, the other loses. One-Card Two-Round Poker from the last
    section is an example. Here's its payoff matrix again (the reduced game
    from Section 5.4):
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-poker-zero-sum">
    <title>One-Card Two-Round Poker, reduced game</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="3">Player 2</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>bb</m></cell>
        <cell><m>bf</m></cell>
        <cell><m>f</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1</cell>
        <cell><m>bb</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>4</m>, <m>-4</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>2</m>, <m>-2</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>bf</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>2</m>, <m>-2</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>f</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>2</m>, <m>-2</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    In every cell, Player 2's payoff is exactly the negative of Player 1's.
    For example, when Player 1 plays <m>bb</m> and Player 2 plays <m>bf</m>,
    Player 1 wins <m>4</m> and Player 2 loses <m>4</m>. That's what makes
    poker zero-sum: every dollar one player wins comes out of the other
    player's pocket. The Battle of the Sexes is not zero-sum. For example,
    in the cell <m>(2, 1)</m>, the payoffs add up to <m>2 + 1 = 3</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The definition only talks about the cells, which come from pure
    strategies. What about mixed strategies?
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-zero-sum-mixed">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the payoffs add up to <m>0</m> in every cell of a game. Why
        do they also add up to <m>0</m> for any pair of <em>mixed</em>
        strategies?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Each player's expected payoff is a weighted average of the cells,
            and both players use the <em>same</em> weights. So the two
            expected payoffs add up to a weighted average of the cell sums,
            which are all <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If a cell has probability <m>w</m>, it contributes
            <m>w \cdot (\text{Player 1's payoff})</m> to <m>\pi_1</m> and
            <m>w \cdot (\text{Player 2's payoff})</m> to <m>\pi_2</m>.
            Adding them, that cell contributes
            <m>w \cdot (\text{cell sum}) = w \cdot 0 = 0</m> to
            <m>\pi_1 + \pi_2</m>. So <m>\pi_1 + \pi_2 = 0</m>, and
            <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m> for mixed strategies too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because each player's expected payoff is <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Individual payoffs don't have to be <m>0</m>. In poker, Player 1
            can win <m>4</m> in a single cell. It's the <em>sum</em> of the
            two players' payoffs that's always <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It isn't always true. It depends on which mixed strategies the
            players use.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            It is always true! Think about how an expected payoff is built
            from the cells, and what the two players' expected payoffs have
            in common.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Here's one more consequence of the definition, which will turn out to be
    the key idea behind this section's main theorem.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-zero-sum-flip">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In a zero-sum game, suppose Player 1 keeps playing the same strategy
        <m>\sigma_1</m>, and Player 2 switches from <m>\sigma_2</m> to some
        other strategy <m>\tau_2</m>. Suppose that
        <me>
          \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) \gt \pi_1(\sigma_1, \tau_2).
        </me>
        In words: Player 1 does worse when Player 2 switches to
        <m>\tau_2</m>. Since the game is zero-sum, what else must be true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) \gt \pi_2(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m>.
            Player 2 also does worse by switching to <m>\tau_2</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Zero-sum means <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m> (for mixed strategies too,
            as we just saw). What happens to an inequality when you multiply
            both sides by <m>-1</m>?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) \lt \pi_2(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m>.
            Player 2 does better by switching to <m>\tau_2</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Since <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m>, multiplying both sides by
            <m>-1</m> flips the inequality. Whatever Player 1 loses, Player 2
            gains. Keep this in mind for the proof later on this page.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    In a zero-sum game, it turns out that maximin equilibria and Nash
    equilibria are exactly the same thing. That's the main theorem of this
    section.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-5-through-theorem">
    <title>Section 5.5, Through Theorem 5.6</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.5 from the beginning through the statement of
      Theorem 5.6.</alert> Stop right before the proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything before the theorem is what we just worked through, so you
      can read that part quickly. You'll see the book's notation for
      everything we did: <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m>, <m>m_1^\dagger</m>, and the
      Battle of the Sexes written in terms of <m>(p, q)</m>. Read the
      statement of Theorem 5.6 carefully, and then we'll break down why it's
      true.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Part 3: why Nash implies maximin                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Let's see why the theorem is true, at least the main part of it. We'll
    work through it in the activity below.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="activity-why-theorem-5-6">
    <title>Why Theorem 5.6 Is True</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium of a
        two-player zero-sum game. We'll show that Player 1's payoff there is
        their maximin payoff:
        <me>
          \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1^\dagger.
        </me>
        We'll do this in two steps: first we'll show
        <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq m_1^\dagger</m>, and then
        we'll show <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq m_1^\dagger</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-proof-step-1">
      <title>Step 1: Show <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq m_1^\dagger</m></title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Take any strategy <m>\sigma_1</m> for Player 1, and compare these three
      payoffs:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>: Player 1's payoff at the
            Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*)</m>: what Player 1 would get by
            switching to <m>\sigma_1</m> while Player 2 sticks with
            <m>\sigma_2^*</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m>: Player 1's worst case when they play
            <m>\sigma_1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="check-proof-step1-order">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which chain of inequalities is correct?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="no">
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*) \geq m_1(\sigma_1)</m></p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Correct. The first inequality is because
                <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium, so
                Player 1 can't do better by switching to <m>\sigma_1</m>. The
                second is because <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m> is the
                <em>smallest</em> payoff Player 1 can get from
                <m>\sigma_1</m>, so it's no bigger than their payoff against
                <m>\sigma_2^*</m> in particular.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement>
              <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*) \geq \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq m_1(\sigma_1)</m></p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                The first inequality would mean Player 1 does better by
                switching to <m>\sigma_1</m>. What does the definition of a
                Nash equilibrium say about that?
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement>
              <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq m_1(\sigma_1) \geq \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*)</m></p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m> is the <em>smallest</em> payoff Player 1
                can get from <m>\sigma_1</m>, so it can't be bigger than
                <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*)</m>.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
        </choices>
      </task>

      <conclusion>
        <p>
          So <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2^*)
          \geq m_1(\sigma_1)</m>.
          Since <m>\sigma_1</m> was arbitrary, <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>
    is at least as big as every <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m>. So it's at least as
    big as the largest one:
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \geq \max_{\sigma_1} m_1(\sigma_1)
      = m_1^\dagger.
    </me>
    Notice we haven't used the fact that the game is zero-sum yet!
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-proof-step-2">
      <title>Step 2: Show <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq m_1^\dagger</m></title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          This is the step where zero-sum matters. Suppose Player 1 keeps
          playing <m>\sigma_1^*</m>, but Player 2 switches to some other
          strategy <m>\sigma_2</m>, and Player 1 does worse:
          <me>
            \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \gt \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2).
          </me>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="check-proof-zero-sum-flip">
        <statement>
          <p>
            This is exactly the situation from
            <xref ref="check-zero-sum-flip"/>, before the reading: Player 1
            keeps the same strategy (<m>\sigma_1^*</m>), and Player 2
            switches from <m>\sigma_2^*</m> to <m>\sigma_2</m>. Using what
            you found there, what must be true?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="no">
          <choice>
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \gt \pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)</m>.
                Player 2 also does worse by switching to <m>\sigma_2</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Look back at <xref ref="check-zero-sum-flip"/>. In a zero-sum
                game, when Player 1 does worse, what happens to Player 2?
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \lt \pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)</m>.
                Player 2 does better by switching to <m>\sigma_2</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Correct. Whatever Player 1 loses, Player 2 gains. But
                <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium, so
                Player 2 <em>can't</em> do better by switching. That's a
                contradiction, so this situation can never happen.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
        </choices>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="check-proof-conclude-m1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Part (i) leads to a contradiction: Player 2 would do better by
            switching, but at a Nash equilibrium they can't. So Player 1 can
            never do worse when Player 2 switches. In other words,
            <me>
              \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2)
              \quad\text{for every } \sigma_2.
            </me>
            This is really the heart of the theorem: in a zero-sum game,
            Player 2's best response is the move that hurts Player 1 the
            most. What can we conclude from this inequality?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="no">
          <choice>
            <statement>
              <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1^\dagger</m></p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                That's where we're headed, but we're not there yet. This
                inequality only compares payoffs where Player 1 plays
                <m>\sigma_1^*</m>. So it tells us about the worst case for
                <m>\sigma_1^*</m> specifically, <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m>, not the
                best worst case over all strategies, <m>m_1^\dagger</m>.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement>
              <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) \leq m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m>, but not necessarily equal.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                This is true, but we can say more. Is
                <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> itself one of the payoffs
                Player 1 can get when they play <m>\sigma_1^*</m>?
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p><m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m></p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Correct. Among all the payoffs Player 1 can get by playing
                <m>\sigma_1^*</m>, <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is less
                than or equal to every one of them, <em>and</em> it's one of them
                (the one where Player 2 plays <m>\sigma_2^*</m>). So it's the
                smallest one, which is exactly <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*)</m>. In other
                words, <m>\sigma_2^*</m> is Player 1's worst case.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
        </choices>
      </task>

      <conclusion>
        <p>
    To finish Step 2: <m>m_1^\dagger</m> is the largest of all the
    <m>m_1(\sigma_1)</m>, so in particular
    <m>m_1(\sigma_1^*) \leq m_1^\dagger</m>. Therefore
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1(\sigma_1^*) \leq m_1^\dagger.
    </me>
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Putting it together</title>
    <p>
      Steps 1 and 2 together give
      <me>
        \pi_1(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_1(\sigma_1^*) = m_1^\dagger.
      </me>
      The same argument works for Player 2, so
      <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*) = m_2(\sigma_2^*) = m_2^\dagger</m>.
      Here's what that tells us:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Every Nash equilibrium is a maximin equilibrium. That's half of part (1) of
            the theorem.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            At the Nash equilibrium, each player gets their maximin payoff.
            That's part (2).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Both maximin payoffs come from the same strategy profile
            <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>, and the payoffs there add to
            <m>0</m> because the game is zero-sum. So
            <m>m_1^\dagger + m_2^\dagger = 0</m>. That's part (3). (This also
            uses the fact that a Nash equilibrium exists in the first place,
            which Nash's Existence Theorem, Theorem 5.1, guarantees.)
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      The other direction of part (1), that every maximin equilibrium is a
      Nash equilibrium, is also true. The book proves it using part (3), if
      you're curious.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <p>
    We'll finish Section 5.5 on the next page, where we'll see what Theorem
    5.6 tells us about One-Card Two-Round Poker.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-4" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.4" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
